## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical experience with event-driven pipelines, schema evolution, and reliable read-model updates in real-world systems.

## Key Concepts

- Event sourcing, Kafka at-least-once vs exactly-once, Schema Registry compatibility
- Idempotent consumers, replay/backfill strategies, fault injection
- Observability, test data generation, and staged rollouts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test to validate idempotent read-model update
function testIdempotentUpdate(eventStream) {
  const before = readModel.snapshot();
  applyEvents(eventStream);
  const after = readModel.snapshot();
  assert.deepEqual(before, after, "idempotent updates must yield same state on replay");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and handle schema drift in the registry?
- How would you validate backfill correctness when the read model is rebuilt?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's expertise in testing complex streaming systems with stringent privacy and availability requirements. It probes understanding of end-to-end data pipeline validation, privacy compliance, and operational resilience.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency and availability in distributed streaming systems
- Privacy controls including PII masking and data retention policies
- Data drift detection and disaster recovery strategies
- Synthetic data generation and test automation patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for PII masking logic
describe('PIIMasking', () => {
  const masking = new PIIMasking();

  test('masks email addresses completely', () => {
    const input = { user_id: '123', email: 'john.doe@example.com' };
    const result = masking.mask(input);
    expect(result.email).toBe('j***@***.com');
    expect(result.user_id).toBe('123');
  });

  test('handles nested PII objects', () => {
    const input = { profile: { name: 'Jane', ssn: '123-45-6789' } };
    const result = masking.mask(input);
    expect(result.profile.ssn).toBe('***-**-6789');
  });
});

// Integration test for Kafka-Flink-TimescaleDB flow
describe('PipelineIntegration', () => {
  test('processes events within 200ms latency', async () => {
    const start = Date.now();
    await produceTestEvent({ user_id: '123', action: 'click' });
    await waitForProcessedMetric('123');
    const latency = Date.now() - start;
    expect(latency).toBeLessThan(200);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design practical testing strategies for common API patterns involving user signup, rate limiting, and idempotency. It tests understanding of testing methodologies, external service mocking, and CI/CD considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation testing
- Idempotency key patterns
- Rate limiting verification
- External service mocking strategies
- Retry mechanisms and failure simulation
- CI/CD integration testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Idempotency implementation sketch
function signup(req, res, db, emailService) {
  const idempotencyKey = req.headers['Idempotency-Key'];

  // Check for existing request
  const existingResult = await db.getResult(idempotencyKey);
  if (existingResult) {
    return res.status(200).json(existingResult);
  }

  // Validate input and create user
  const user = await validateAndCreateUser(req.body);

  // Send email with retry logic
  try {
    await emailService.sendVerification(user.email);
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle email failure with retry queue
  }

  // Store result with idempotency key
  const result = { userId: user.id, status: 'created' };
  await db.storeResult(idempotencyKey, result);

  return res.status(201).json(result);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the testing strategy for a microservices architecture with distributed signup services?
- What considerations would you add for testing GDPR compliance and data privacy requirements?

